OpenBSD VMs

It needs following firmwares:

    pkg_add u-boot-aarch64 u-boot-arm u-boot-octeon u-boot-riscv64 opensbi talos-ii-pnor-bootkernel

With QEMU 11.1.0 and libslirp 4.9.3, it needs following patches which is
included itno OpenBSD QEMU port:

    sparc64:
      https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-tech&m=178126606643187&q=raw
      https://github.com/catap/openbios/commit/9151118aea489f9fffa40c60ee3b1d93383fb96b.patch
      https://github.com/catap/openbios/commit/8d5ed09f0420389a27394b7c1b7d40ae7aab9d32.patch
      https://github.com/catap/openbios/commit/cd90de7bda9006e2382d10302555a4cf825ea425.patch
      https://github.com/catap/openbios/commit/daa1b2595b2347e8031431dae1aeded976fd7057.patch

    powerpc64:
      https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-tech&m=178101765759037&q=raw
      https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-tech&m=178394830014223&q=raw
      https://patchew.org/QEMU/20260719145559.94342-1-kirill@korins.ky/

    OpenBSD/armv7:
      https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-tech&m=178111370945923&q=raw

    QEMU/octeon:
      https://patchew.org/QEMU/20260719215455.96122-1-kirill@korins.ky/
      https://patchew.org/QEMU/20260715134447.56007-1-kirill@korins.ky/

    OpenBSD/macppc:
      https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-tech&m=178514780514282&w=2
      https://patchew.org/QEMU/20260827155310.7223-1-kirill@korins.ky/
      https://github.com/catap/openbios/commit/e61a69213260e7eae8bef4a8ef0ce5c0b0f6be8a.patch
      https://github.com/catap/openbios/commit/d14f3c85b567048f0c756b7b030427245416e23d.patch
      https://github.com/catap/openbios/commit/c05a3d608e452276045e7ec8804a235f8e710e5f.patch
      https://github.com/catap/openbios/commit/06bd9d43e0755027cad82ba165cb3d2fdd3137a9.patch
      https://github.com/catap/openbios/commit/d8d459af79ae177dd167be3909ef6f0fab91a563.patch
      https://github.com/catap/openbios/commit/ba2f9b93d4a157f6d29df606ec4abf6f605e7187.patch

    libslirp:
      https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/slirp/libslirp/-/commit/3e6e68e779c9051590250a87214dabf91e70949a.patch

Run a VM:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] <arch> [qemu args ...]

Boot miniroot with attached VM disk:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] -m (-s|-R version) <arch> [installurl] [qemu args ...]

Autoinstall or autoupgrade over TFTP:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] -i (-s|-R version) <arch> [installurl] [qemu args ...]
    ./obsd.sh [-n] -u (-s|-R version) <arch> [installurl] [qemu args ...]

Shrink a qcow2 image:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] -z <arch> [qemu-img convert args ...]

Use -c after <arch> to create a compressed qcow2. Zero free space inside
the guest first if you want the conversion to reclaim by Boot the guest
as /bsd -s, then run:

    fsck -p && mount -uw / && mount -a -t ffs && mount -t ffs | while read dev on mp rest; do dd if=/dev/zero of=$mp/.zero bs=1m; rm -f $mp/.zero; done; sync; halt

With -n, the script prints the command instead of running it.

DISK_SIZE and SSH_PORT env override the default VM disk size (100G) and
host SSH forwarding port (22022).

The dummy HTTP server is used only by autoinstall and autoupgrade; it
serves install.conf and disklabel from the current directory.

Some arches may not support TFTP install like sparc64, in that case -m
starts dummy HTTP server as well.

On octeon, -m, -i, and -u use u-boot-octeon3-obsd-install.pflash to
load the TFTP bootfile. Normal boots use
u-boot-octeon3-obsd-boot.pflash to load boot from usb 0:1. Both
pflash images hardcode numcores=4 and pass ${loadaddr} to
bootoctlinux; the install pflash also passes rootdev=em0 when the DHCP
bootfile is not bsd.rd, so autoinstall and autoupgrade are seen as
network boots.

On armv7, the target disk has an MSDOS boot partition visible as i: in
the disklabel. Autopartitioning has room for at most 14 OpenBSD
partitions there.

On macppc, OpenBIOS cannot boot bsd.rd directly or through TFTP, so only
-m and normal run mode are supported. -m boots cd*.iso with the target
disk at IDE index 0 and the CD at IDE index 2. QEMU's mac99 machine
supports one CPU and at most 2G of RAM. The required QEMU patches fix
OpenPIC delivery, decrementer interrupts, and CUDA replies, and expose
cuda.sr-delay-ns. The launcher sets that delay to zero because OpenBSD
host timer granularity otherwise breaks long replies. The ADB keyboard,
CUDA RTC, and e1000 attach; e1000 obtains a DHCP lease. Normal boots load
hd:,ofwboot /bsd explicitly.

Not supported:

    alpha
    sh4 (landisk)
    m88k (luna88k)

alpha is blocked by QEMU firmware support. QEMU only provides the
clipper machine with emulation PALcode, not an SRM console able to boot
OpenBSD media. Direct -kernel loading bypasses the OpenBSD boot path and
does not provide the PROM state OpenBSD expects.

sh4 means OpenBSD/landisk. QEMU only provides r2d-plus, not the IO-DATA
LANDISK platform. Disk boot lacks LANDISK firmware, and direct kernel
entry resets before the console is initialized.

m88k means OpenBSD/luna88k. QEMU does not provide an m88k system
emulator.
